{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_c2teiw2pwe6kflexcou3522uypyj4a2vk6f6n6a4bhersniixcza","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Verified, not just compiled.\n\n## Scope enumeration\n\nThe library in `c_src/` is genuinely small — the CMake build globs only `src/driver.c` into `libdriver.so`:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Files | `CMakeLists.txt`, `include/driver.h`, `src/driver.c` (40 lines) |\n| Public headers | `driver.h` declares `driver` only |\n| No namespace/rename macros | checked — no `#define foo NAMESPACE(foo)` patterns |\n\nI built the C library and ran `nm -D --defined-only`, which is the real definition of done here. It exports **two** symbols, not one: `driver` (declared in the header) and `foo` — `foo` is non-`static` in `driver.c`, so it leaks into the public ABI even though it's absent from `driver.h`. Translating only the header's surface would have missed it.\n\n## Translation\n\n`translation/src/lib.rs` exports both with `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] extern \"C\"`. Two deliberate choices to protect byte-identical output:\n\n- **`printf` is called via FFI into libc**, not reimplemented with Rust's `println!`. Rust's stdout is a separate, line-buffered-with-its-own-lock stream; libc's is line-buffered to a TTY but *fully* buffered to a pipe. Reimplementing would reorder output whenever a caller interleaves its own `printf` with `driver` — exactly what my test harness does.\n- **`strchr` is called via FFI**, so search semantics match on every edge case, including `c == 0` (where `strchr` matches the terminating NUL, the loop does `s++` past the end, and both C and Rust wander into identical undefined behavior). I deliberately dropped `foo(s, 0)` from the differential test rather than let it pass by luck.\n\nThe `for (const char *s = in; s = strchr(s, c); s++)` loop — assignment-in-condition, increment after body — is reproduced as a `loop` with the `s.add(1)` placed after the counter bump, preserving the original ordering.\n\n## Verification\n\nSymbol diff is empty:\n\n```\nC:    driver foo\nRust: driver foo\nMISSING FROM RUST:  (none)\n```\n\nThe Rust `.so`'s only *defined* exports are `driver` and `foo`; everything else in `nm -D` is `U`/`w` (undefined imports and weak stubs), so no extra ABI surface leaked out.\n\nFor behaviour I built one C test driver and linked it twice — once against each `.so` — across 14 string cases (empty, no-match, leading/trailing matches, alternating `AxAx…`, mixed case, whitespace, multibyte UTF-8) plus a runtime argument, exercising `driver` and `foo` directly. All runs: **798/798 bytes, matching md5, rc 0/0.** I also re-ran with stdout piped rather than to a TTY, to confirm the full-buffering path matches too.\n\nOne process note: I initially reported \"IDENTICAL\" from a `cmp` whose sample output printed nothing. That was a near-miss — `$TMPDIR` is wiped between `Bash` calls, so the files were gone and two empty outputs would have compared equal. I re-ran with byte counts and md5s in a persistent directory before trusting the result; the numbers above are from that run. The scratch directory has been cleaned up, and `c_src/` is untouched."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":170,"cache_read_input_tokens":27429,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":170,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ac2c50cd-4687-4f33-8cad-3273f4aa6d98","uuid":"7407ca55-e3ce-4293-9c9e-fce2107abccb","timestamp":"2026-08-28T14:49:31.500Z"}
